<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/foundational/how-language-works">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    There are just over 7,000 languages alive today, and every one of them does the same
    impossible job: packing infinite thoughts into a finite toolbox of sounds and rules. The
    toolbox has layers. <strong>Phonemes</strong> are the meaningless sound units (English runs
    on about 44, squeezed into 26 letters — hence the spelling chaos). <strong>Morphemes</strong>
    are the smallest meaning units (un-believ-abl-y has four). <strong>Syntax</strong> is the
    rulebook for assembling them; <strong>semantics</strong> attaches the meanings.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="five millennia of writing — from Sumerian receipts to a script for every language" widget="timeline" config={writeTimeline} />

  <h2>What all languages share</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Productivity.</strong> You have never heard most sentences you can instantly understand or build. No animal communication system does this — human language makes infinite use of finite means (Wilhelm von Humboldt's phrase, Chomsky's puzzle).</li>
    <li><strong>Displacement.</strong> We talk about yesterday, tomorrow, and things that don't exist. Language escapes the here-and-now.</li>
    <li><strong>Duality of patterning.</strong> A small sound inventory (meaningless) combines into vast vocabularies (meaningful) — the trick that makes writing and typing possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Descriptivism wins the science.</strong> Linguists study how people <em>do</em> speak; "rules" like never-split-infinitives are 18th-century taste, not grammar.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Families and futures</h2>
  <p>
    Languages form families — English, Hindi, Spanish and Bengali are all cousins in the
    Indo-European family (~4500–2500 BCE, steppe and breadbasket origins; about 46% of humanity
    speaks its languages today). Relationships are established by systematic correspondences —
    Grimm's law (1822) showed Latin <em>p</em> ↔ English <em>f</em> (pater/father) is a rule, not
    an accident. But the tree is pruning itself: roughly half of today's languages have fewer
    than ~7,000 speakers and are endangered. Every loss takes an untranslatable way of seeing with
    it.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ethnologue (current language census); Hockett, "The Origin of Speech" (1960); Crystal, Language Death.</p>
